FINDINGS -- same Amdahl inversion as the A40 (results_g0_mpi_gpu), now on 2 GPUs
  1. The GPU already erases the O(N^2) regular force at np1 (P7 = 8.2 s of a
     287 s wall). There is almost nothing left for more GPUs to take.
  2. np2 nets only 1.05x: P3 halves (91.8->49.2) and P7 genuinely drops on two
     GPUs (8.2->6.1), saving ~45 s -- but ~10 s of new Allgather (P3c+P7c) and
     ~17 s of extra DRAM contention on the REPLICATED phases (predict,
     corrector, reg-tail, BSE) eat most of it. Net SUM 235->219.
  3. np4 is COUNTERPRODUCTIVE (0.92x): two ranks per GPU serialize kernel
     launches + j-sends (P7 8.2->13.2), comm triples (28 s), and the replicated
     block climbs to 153 s. Adding ranks past one-per-GPU only adds tax.
  4. The wall is dominated by replicated, memory-bound CPU phases that GROW
     with rank count -- the copy-algorithm bandwidth tax, identical to the
     gpudyn3 mpi-cpu np16 finding. At this N the multi-GPU lever is too small
     to overcome it.
  5. Implication: 2-GPU throughput will pay off at LARGER N / production DTADJ,
     where P3+P7 (O(N^2)) again dominate the wall and the replicated O(N) phases
     are relatively cheap (cf. G0 NEXT item c). For N~1e4 BSE runs, np2 (strict
     1 rank/GPU) is the only config that helps, and only marginally; np1 is
     entirely competitive. Do NOT oversubscribe GPUs (np > nGPU).

GOTCHA (harness, not integrator)
  First sweep lost np2's phase table to a bash bug: `local np=$1 d=$W/np$np`
  expands $np BEFORE the assignment takes effect (empty), so np2 and np4 both
  used $W/np and np4's rm -rf wiped np2. Wall (from the echo) survived; re-ran
  np2 alone to recover the table above. Fix: separate `local` lines, or don't
  reference a just-declared var in the same `local`.
